---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/company.css';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/Hunter-124/Chudware';
---
<BaseLayout title="Source and licenses" description="Chudware's MIT software rights, current source availability, free self-hosting, optional paid hosting, and the licenses of its third-party components.">
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Open source</p>
      <h1>MIT-licensed software. Your choice of deployment.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Use, study, change, and redistribute Chudware under the MIT terms. Desktop use, source builds, and browser self-hosting have no Chudware software fee. Clone the public source repository to build and run it yourself; public desktop installers are not yet available.</p>
      <dl class="co-sec-facts">
        <div><dt>License</dt><dd>MIT</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Source</dt><dd>Hunter-124/Chudware (public)</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Hosting</dt><dd>Self-host free; optional paid operation</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="os-project">
    <div class="wrap co-about-body">
      <div class="co-prose">
        <h2 id="os-project" class="co-anchor">What the repository contains</h2>
        <p>The C++ desktop and WebAssembly interfaces and native geometry engine, Python gateway, worker, and tool services, packaging scripts for Windows, Linux, and macOS, and release workflows. Browser modeling uses the native C++ engine on the server, with Python providing supporting services.</p>
        <p>The <a class="text-link" href="/docs/self-hosting/">self-hosting guide</a> builds a single-operator browser deployment from a source checkout. Optional paid, owner-operated hosting supports development; <a class="text-link" href="/contact/">ask about hosting</a> without changing your MIT software rights.</p>

        <h2>License</h2>
        <p>The MIT License permits use, copying, modification, merging, publication, distribution, sublicensing, and sale of the software, provided the copyright notice and the permission notice are included in all copies or substantial portions of it. The software is provided without warranty. The repository's <a class="text-link" href={`${REPO}/blob/HEAD/LICENSE`}>LICENSE</a> file is the governing text.</p>

        <h2>Contributing</h2>
        <p>Use <a class="text-link" href={`${REPO}/issues`}>GitHub issues</a>, pull requests, and the <a class="text-link" href={`${REPO}/blob/HEAD/CONTRIBUTING.md`}>contributing guide</a> in the public repository. For private security disclosure, <a class="text-link" href="/contact/">contact the project</a>. Never include secrets or confidential designs in a public report.</p>
      </div>

      <aside class="co-aside" aria-labelledby="os-repository">
        <p class="eyebrow" id="os-repository">Repository</p>
        <dl class="co-spec">
          <div><dt>Project</dt><dd>Hunter-124/Chudware</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Languages</dt><dd>C++ and Python</dd></div>
          <div><dt>CAD kernel</dt><dd>OpenCASCADE</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Interface</dt><dd>Dear ImGui</dd></div>
          <div><dt>License</dt><dd>MIT</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/docs/self-hosting/">Self-hosting guide</a><a class="button button-secondary" href={`${REPO}#building-from-source`}>Native build guide</a></div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="os-components">
    <div class="wrap">
      <header class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Built on open source</p>
        <h2 id="os-components" class="co-anchor">Key third-party components.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Chudware stands on established open-source projects. Each component keeps its own license terms.</p>
      </header>
      <ol class="co-controls">
        <li>
          <span class="co-controls-index">01</span>
          <div><h3><a class="text-link" href="https://dev.opencascade.org/">OpenCASCADE Technology</a></h3><span class="co-source">LGPL-2.1 with the OCCT exception</span></div>
          <p>The exact B-rep geometry kernel: solids, Booleans, fillets, and STEP and IGES exchange. Linked dynamically.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="co-controls-index">02</span>
          <div><h3><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/ocornut/imgui">Dear ImGui</a></h3><span class="co-source">MIT</span></div>
          <p>The immediate-mode UI toolkit behind the desktop interface, with GLFW for windows, input, and the OpenGL context.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="co-controls-index">03</span>
          <div><h3><a class="text-link" href="https://eigen.tuxfamily.org/">Eigen</a></h3><span class="co-source">MPL-2.0</span></div>
          <p>Dense and sparse linear algebra for the simulation solvers and geometry code, with Spectra for eigenvalue problems.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="co-controls-index">04</span>
          <div><h3><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/Hunter-124/Polyhedral-FEA">PolyMesh</a></h3><span class="co-source">BSD-3-Clause</span></div>
          <p>CAD-aware volume meshing in the FEA path, pinned to a public commit. The integration is described below.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="supporting-copy">Also used: nlohmann/json, Clipper2, miniz, stb, openNURBS, lib3mf, and the Chromium Embedded Framework.</p>
      <p class="supporting-copy">AI is optional. Desktop Copilot needs its configured agent runtime and your own supported provider account or API key; provider charges and privacy terms apply. The browser container does not require that runtime, and online browser Copilot is unavailable. See <a class="text-link" href="/docs/ai-copilot/">AI setup and limits</a>. Distributed components retain their license notices whether or not AI is enabled.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="os-polymesh">
    <div class="wrap co-about-body">
      <div class="co-prose">
        <h2 id="os-polymesh" class="co-anchor">PolyMesh, the FEA mesher</h2>
        <p><strong>Polyhedral-FEA</strong>, also called PolyMesh, is a BSD-3-Clause CAD meshing and linear-elastostatics project. It imports STEP and B-rep geometry, builds hybrid volume meshes, assembles standard finite elements and polyhedral Virtual Element Method cells into one global system, solves small-strain linear elasticity, recovers stress, estimates error, and writes VTU result fields.</p>
        <p>The adaptive path can alter local size and element order from geometry demand and recovery-based error. A learned advisor can rank measured candidate actions within a degrees-of-freedom budget, but it remains a chooser with refusal gates—not an accuracy guarantee.</p>
        <p>The repository ships command-line, desktop-study, and self-hosted browser applications in addition to the headless libraries, with build and use instructions maintained alongside the source.</p>
      </div>

      <aside class="co-aside" aria-labelledby="os-polymesh-repository">
        <p class="eyebrow" id="os-polymesh-repository">PolyMesh repository</p>
        <dl class="co-spec">
          <div><dt>Project</dt><dd>Hunter-124/Polyhedral-FEA</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Language</dt><dd>C++20</dd></div>
          <div><dt>CAD kernel</dt><dd>OpenCASCADE</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Linear algebra</dt><dd>Eigen</dd></div>
          <div><dt>License</dt><dd>BSD 3-Clause</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Evidence</dt><dd>Tests, reports, result JSON, and showcase manifests in the repository</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <div class="actions"><a class="button button-secondary" href="https://github.com/Hunter-124/Polyhedral-FEA">Read the PolyMesh source</a></div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="os-integration">
    <div class="wrap">
      <header class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Inside Chudware</p>
        <h2 id="os-integration" class="co-anchor">The integration boundary is explicit.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Chudware's build fetches an immutable public PolyMesh commit and links its meshing pipeline in process. Packaged results identify the PolyMesh commit so the meshing input remains traceable.</p>
      </header>
      <ol class="co-controls">
        <li>
          <span class="co-controls-index">01</span>
          <div><h3>Volume meshing</h3><span class="co-source">PolyMesh pipeline</span></div>
          <p>Chudware supplies exact exported geometry, size controls, feature-resolution requirements, and resource ceilings. PolyMesh returns the volume mesh consumed by the study.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="co-controls-index">02</span>
          <div><h3>Adaptive path</h3><span class="co-source">mesh → solve → estimate → refine</span></div>
          <p>Adaptive static studies feed recovered-error information back into the meshing path. The reported indicator guides refinement but does not certify the physical model.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="co-controls-index">03</span>
          <div><h3>Study and solver layer</h3><span class="co-source">Chudware engine</span></div>
          <p>Chudware owns face selection, materials, interface policies, resource admission, its static, contact, modal, thermal and buckling solvers, result reduction, evidence fields, and document persistence.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="co-controls-index">04</span>
          <div><h3>Provenance</h3><span class="co-source">returned with results</span></div>
          <p>Engine build, PolyMesh commit, solver, mesher, and timestamp travel with result evidence rather than being inferred from the currently installed application.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="os-evidence">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <header class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Published verification</p>
        <h2 id="os-evidence" class="co-anchor">Measured against analytical cases and committed baselines.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The public PolyMesh repository reports the setup, tolerance, and artifact behind each result. Representative analytical results in the current project README include:</p>
        <ul class="fact-list">
          <li>Lamé thick-cylinder inner-wall radial displacement: 0.0068% error; hoop stress: 1.36% error</li>
          <li>Kirsch plate-with-hole stress concentration: 3.056 versus 3.0, or 1.87% error</li>
          <li>Timoshenko cantilever tip deflection: 1.50% error</li>
          <li>Goodier spherical-cavity stress concentration: 7.04% error against the stated reference</li>
        </ul>
      </header>
      <div class="co-note">
        <p class="eyebrow">Adaptive comparisons</p>
        <p>The public L-domain artifact reports 1,248 versus 6,384 degrees of freedom and 0.227 versus 2.762 seconds against the project's own frozen uniform-tet10 baseline. The matched-648-DOF Kirsch comparison reports 0.70% versus 3.06% stress-concentration error for feature-aware logarithmic grading versus linear grading.</p>
        <p>Those are self-relative, scoped comparisons, not a claim that PolyMesh is faster or more accurate than every external solver. The repository publishes the baselines and comparison limits with the numbers.</p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/Hunter-124/Polyhedral-FEA#measured-results">Open the measured results</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="os-limits">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <header class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Published limits</p>
        <h2 id="os-limits" class="co-anchor">Validation scope and product scope are not interchangeable.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The project's analytical accuracy cases use documented structured parametric meshes. They do not, by themselves, establish the same error on every CAD-derived product mesh.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="co-note co-note-warn">
        <ul class="fact-list">
          <li>The standalone analysis scope is linear elastostatics; Chudware's thermal, modal, contact, buckling, and fatigue paths belong to Chudware.</li>
          <li>The default Chudware product path uses graded tetrahedra. Polyhedral VEM product transitions remain gated.</li>
          <li>Extreme slivers and high-order iterative solves have documented convergence limits.</li>
          <li>The learned advisor selects or refuses among measured actions; it does not guarantee a requested tolerance or complete out-of-distribution detection.</li>
          <li>Feature resolution, CAD boundary projection, and resource ceilings can cause a mesh to refuse instead of returning an under-resolved result.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section cta-band">
    <div class="wrap section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Inspect the source</p>
      <h2>Read the instructions, choose your setup.</h2>
      <p class="lede">The public repository is the authority for build instructions. The website guides explain deployment choices and getting started without requiring a Chudware account.</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/docs/self-hosting/">Self-hosting guide</a><a class="button button-secondary" href="/contact/">Ask about hosting</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
